	Write data to a channel configured for data communications
apifn	smdc_tx_data
params	smdc_data_parms datap
	The data communication channel.
field	tSMChannelId channel in
	A pointer to the data to be written.
field	char* data in rxtx_data
	The amount of data in the buffer.
field	tSM_INT max_length in
	The amount of data actually written.
field	tSM_INT done_length out
unstruct
	Attempt to write data to a channel whose output half has been
	configured for a data commuications protocol. Less than the
	maximum will be written when either:
	<ul>
	<li>there is a status change which must be checked
	<li>there is insufficient space and this function has been 
	forbidden to wait by the
	<a href="apifn-smdc_tx_control.html#field-blocking">blocking</a>
	value set with
fnref	smdc_tx_control.
	</ul>
	<p>
	To permit maximum system throughput, this function refuses to
	write to a channel whose status has changed since it was last
	checked. This allows an application to avoid checking the
	status during bulk data transfer. The application only needs
	to check the status (using
fnref	smdc_tx_status)
	when a call to
fnref	smdc_tx_data
	returns with
ref	done_length
	equal to zero. If a status change occurs while writing data,
ref	done_length
	may indicate that some, but not all, of the data was written.
	In this case, the most convenient way for an application to
	react is often to note how much data has been sent, and to
	try to send more. Since the new attempt will indicate that
	no data was written, it means that the application does not
	need to have different cases to handle a status change at the
	beginning of the data buffer and a status change after a
	partial write.
	<p>
	See also the document <a href="../prosdc_config.html">Prosody
	Data Communcations Protocols and Encodings</a>
	for details of behaviour specific to individual protocols and
	encodings.
