	The amount of data actually read.
field	tSM_INT done_length out
unstruct
	Attempt to read data from a channel whose input half has been
	configured for a data communications protocol. Less than the
	maximum will be read when either:
	<ul>
	<li>there is a status change which must be checked
	<li>there is no more data to read
	</ul>
	<p>
	To permit maximum system throughput, this function refuses to
	read from a channel whose status has changed since it was last
	checked. This allows an application to avoid checking the
	status during bulk data transfer. The application only needs
	to check the status when a call to
fnref	smdc_rx_data
	returns with
ref	done_length
	equal to zero. This also ensures that status changes are
	reported at the point in the data at which they occurred,
	which may be important for some applications.
	<p>
	If an error is reported, the
ref	done_length
	field indicates the amount of data which was read before the
	error occurred.
	<p>
	Any amount of data (up to
ref	max_length)
	may be returned - even less than configured with the
	<a href="apifn-smdc_rx_control.html#field-min_to_collect">min_to_collect</a>,
	parameter set with
fnref	smdc_rx_control,
	which only controls when the channel is declared ready. Once
	a channel is ready, it may remain so until all the data is
	read from it. This means that if the first read does not read
	all the data (i.e. the buffer supplied is too small), the
	next read will return immediately with more.
	<p>
	See also the document <a href="../prosdc_config.html">Prosody
	Data Communcations Protocols and Encodings</a>
	for details of behaviour specific to individual protocols and
	encodings.
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

	Stop data communications protocols on a channel.
apifn	smdc_stop
params	smdc_stop_parms pp
	The data communication channel.
field	tSMChannelId channel in
unstruct
	This stops any protocol configured on a channel. If the input
	half and the output half are configured with independent
	protocols, both are stopped. Unlike the
	<a href="apifn-smdc_line_control.html#field-kSMDCLineCtlCmdDisconnect">
	kSMDCLineCtlCmdDisconnect</a> command of
fnref	smdc_line_control,
	this does not use any disconnection procedures of the
	protocols - it simply stops immediately. It is intended for
	situations where there is no need to disconnect properly,
	such as when call control indicates that the remote party has
	cleared the call.
	<p>
	This function is equivalent to calling both
fnref	smdc_rx_stop
	and
fnref	smdc_tx_stop
	on the channel, except that it does not report an error if only one
	half of the channel is configured with a protocol.
	<p>
	When the input half of a channel has stopped,
fnref	smdc_rx_status
	returns the status
	<a href="apifn-smdc_rx_status.html#field-kSMDCRxStatusFinished">
	kSMDCRxStatusFinished</a>.
	When the output half of a channel has stopped
fnref	smdc_tx_status
	returns the status
	<a href="apifn-smdc_tx_status.html#field-kSMDCTxStatusFinished">
	kSMDCTxStatusFinished</a>.
	<p>
	See also the document <a href="../prosdc_config.html">Prosody
	Data Communcations Protocols and Encodings</a>
	for details of behaviour specific to individual protocols and
	encodings.
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

